            "No study can measure everyone, so every quantitative study measures a <strong>sample</strong> and uses it to say something about a <strong>population</strong>, and the quality of the study depends on how honestly that step was taken. The <strong>target population</strong> is the whole group to whom the researcher wants the findings to apply — all nurses in Ghana's district hospitals; all women delivering in the Volta Region; all children under five in the Northern Region. The <strong>accessible population</strong> is the part of it the researcher can actually reach — the nurses of the four district hospitals in one region, the women delivering at Ho Teaching Hospital in the study year — and the difference between the two is the first limitation of any project. The <strong>sampling frame</strong> is the list from which the sample is drawn — the duty roster, the delivery register, the electoral register, the community health volunteer's household list — and a frame that is out of date or incomplete excludes people before the study begins. The <strong>sampling unit</strong> is the thing selected — a person, a household, a ward, a school — and the <strong>element</strong> is the thing measured, which may differ (households are sampled; children in them are weighed).",
