            "A questionnaire is an instrument, and a poorly built one measures nothing. It begins with a short <strong>introduction</strong> stating who is asking, why, how long it takes, that participation is voluntary and how confidentiality is kept — the information sheet of lesson 4.1 in miniature — and ends with thanks. The items are ordered from easy and general to difficult and sensitive, grouped by topic, with demographic questions at the end (or the beginning, if they are needed to route the respondent) rather than opening with age, income and religion. <strong>Closed questions</strong> offer fixed responses — yes/no, a list of categories that are <strong>exhaustive</strong> (every possible answer has a box, including ‘other, please specify’) and <strong>mutually exclusive</strong> (no respondent fits two boxes: age bands ‘20–30’ and ‘30–40’ fail this test) — and are quick to answer and code; <strong>open questions</strong> let the respondent answer in her own words, give richer data and surprises, and cost far more to analyse, so they are used sparingly at the end of a section. The <strong>Likert scale</strong> presents a statement and asks for the degree of agreement on a five- or seven-point scale (strongly disagree to strongly agree), with a neutral midpoint or deliberately without one; its items are scored and summed into a total, half of them are worded in the opposite direction to catch the respondent who ticks the same column throughout, and the sum is an <strong>ordinal</strong> measure (lesson 5.1) however much it is treated as interval. Other formats include the <strong>rating scale</strong> (pain from 0 to 10), the <strong>visual analogue scale</strong>, ranking, and the <strong>knowledge test</strong> with one correct answer per item and a score out of the total.",
