          alt: "Surveillance: the ward is where an epidemic is first visible. Under IDSR the whole country reports weekly, and DHIS2 is where it lands. Report these immediately — not weekly, not at the end of the shift: cholera, meningitis, yellow fever, measles, viral haemorrhagic fever, acute flaccid paralysis for polio, Guinea worm, neonatal tetanus, any unusual cluster. A single suspected case is the trigger; you do not wait for laboratory confirmation to notify. Zero reporting is mandatory — if nothing occurred, you report the nothing; a blank return is not a report. What you do, in order: treat and isolate the patient; notify upward at once to the DHMT; record the case with a full address; take the specimen the guideline names; trace and follow the contacts; health education without blame. The three questions of an investigation: person — who is affected, age, sex, occupation; place — where are they, map the cases; time — when did they start, draw the curve. Words examiners use precisely: endemic — always present at a fairly steady level, malaria in most of Ghana; epidemic or outbreak — clearly more cases than expected for that place and season; pandemic — an epidemic crossing countries and continents; sporadic — scattered single cases with no connection. An outbreak is defined against what is expected here, not against zero; two cases can be an outbreak.",
          caption: "Routine data are collected by nurses, every week, under rules: the weekly IDSR return, the zero report and the DHIMS2 entry are the country's largest data set, and the researcher who uses them inherits both their reach and their gaps — the case that was never notified is not in the data.",
