            "<strong>Secondary data</strong> are data collected by someone else for another purpose and re-used for research, and Ghana produces an enormous quantity: the ward admission and discharge registers; the delivery, antenatal, child welfare and family planning registers; the outpatient morbidity returns; the folders; the laboratory and pharmacy records; the weekly Integrated Disease Surveillance and Response returns; the <strong>District Health Information Management System (DHIMS2)</strong>, into which every facility's monthly summary is entered and from which district, regional and national indicators are drawn; the National Health Insurance claims; and the national surveys — the Demographic and Health Survey, the Multiple Indicator Cluster Survey, the census — whose anonymised data sets are free to download. Their strengths are reach, cost and time: a study of maternal deaths over ten years in a regional hospital, of seasonal patterns in meningitis admissions, or of immunisation coverage across 260 districts could be done no other way, and secondary analysis exposes no new participant to risk, which is why ethics committees treat it lightly (though not as nothing — lesson 4.2 returns to records).",
            "Their limits are the limits of the people who filled them in at the end of a night shift. <strong>Completeness</strong>: the folder with no weight, the register with no outcome column filled, the month whose return was never submitted. <strong>Accuracy</strong>: the diagnosis written as ‘malaria’ without a test, the age estimated, the ‘discharged’ that was a death at home. <strong>Consistency of definition</strong>: ‘anaemia’ meaning different thresholds in different years, ‘skilled attendant’ counted differently by different facilities. <strong>Coverage</strong>: the register contains those who came, and the routine data of a district with 60 per cent facility deliveries say nothing about the other 40 per cent. <strong>The denominator problem</strong>: DHIMS2 numerators are counted, but the denominators are population projections from the last census, so that a district whose population has grown, shrunk or crossed to the neighbouring district for its clinic can report coverage above 100 per cent, and the researcher must know how each indicator is built before she interprets it. The researcher using routine data states these limits, checks a sample of entries against the source document where she can, triangulates against another source — the register against the folders, DHIMS2 against the survey — and never reports an indicator whose numerator and denominator she cannot define. Lesson 7.3 shows this data being turned into audit and quality improvement, which is its best use at facility level.",
